+# Hegel property-based testing report — `ttf-parser`
+
+All property tests were added to the existing integration-test file
+`tests/tables/main.rs`, in a new `mod hegel_props`. They use the `hegeltest`
+crate (library name `hegel`, v0.28.2), added as a dev-dependency with
+`cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version` (the crate's `rust-version`
+is 1.63, below hegeltest's MSRV; this is called out here for the maintainers).
+
+`.hegel/` was added to `.gitignore`.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+Twelve properties (10 no-panic/contract properties + the canonical failing
+tests for the three bugs). Each is one `#[hegel::test]`. Evidence for every
+property is in a comment above it.
+
+1. **`tag_to_bytes_from_bytes_roundtrip`** — `Tag::from_bytes(tag.to_bytes()) == tag`
+   for all `u32`, plus the documented `from_bytes_lossy` padding/truncation
+   rules. Evidence: `Tag::{from_bytes,to_bytes}` are inverse bit-packings
+   (src/lib.rs) and the `from_bytes` doc example. PASSES.
+
+2. **`face_parse_never_panics_on_arbitrary_bytes`** — `Face::parse` and
+   `fonts_in_collection` never panic on arbitrary `Vec<u8>` (size drawn
+   separately up to 1KiB); any successfully parsed face is then driven through
+   *every* public accessor (`exercise_face`). Evidence: crate advertises a
+   "safe" parser; the AFL targets in `testing-tools/ttf-fuzz/` assume exactly
+   this. PASSES.
+
+3. **`face_parse_never_panics_on_font_shaped_bytes`** — the "wire-shaped
+   garbage" pattern: a plausible sfnt header + generated table directory
+   (`draw_font_shaped`), with mandatory `head`/`hhea`/`maxp` usually semi-valid
+   so parsing regularly reaches optional-table parsers, table offsets/lengths
+   sometimes honest and sometimes lies, tags drawn from the 42 known tags or
+   random, directory sometimes sorted, and the whole thing sometimes wrapped in
+   a TTC header. Goes beyond the repo's fuzz targets, which feed pure bytes.
+   PASSES (after excluding the two boundary bugs below). This generator is what
+   originally surfaced the `height()` overflow.
+
+4. **`face_never_panics_on_mutated_test_fonts`** — bit-flips, byte writes and
+   truncation of the four real test fonts never panic in parse or any accessor.
+   Evidence: mutated real fonts reach far deeper table code than noise. PASSES.
+
+5. **`regular_font_with_nonzero_index_is_out_of_bounds`** — parsing a
+   non-collection font with any index >= 1 yields `FaceIndexOutOfBounds`.
+   Evidence: `RawFace::parse` treats a regular font "as a one-element
+   collection"; generalizes the `font_index_overflow_on_regular_font` unit
+   test. PASSES.
+
+6. **`font_collection_index_contract`** — for a generated TTC of n real faces
+   (offsets rebased correctly): `fonts_in_collection == Some(n)`, every index
+   `< n` parses, every index `>= n` gives `FaceIndexOutOfBounds`. Evidence:
+   `fonts_in_collection` / `FaceIndexOutOfBounds` docs and the
+   `empty_font_collection` / `font_index_overflow` unit tests. PASSES.
+
+7. **`glyph_index_never_returns_glyph_zero`** — documented contract that
+   `glyph_index` and `glyph_variation_index` "return `None` instead of `0`".
+   Runs on pristine and cmap-corrupted fonts, with U+FFFF boosted.
+   **FAILS — real bug (see Suspected bugs #1).**
+
+8. **`table_lookup_agrees_with_linear_directory_scan`** — `RawFace::table`
+   (binary search + slice) agrees with an independent linear scan of
+   `table_records` for every present tag and an arbitrary tag. Evidence:
+   `RawFace::table` docs + the record offset/length fields. PASSES.
+
+9. **`valid_font_queries_never_panic_full_ranges`** — on each pristine test
+   font, `exercise_face` calls every public accessor with full-range arguments
+   (all `u16` glyph ids, full `char` range, arbitrary ppem/palette/tags, finite
+   variation values). Generalizes all three AFL fuzz targets, which probe only a
+   handful of chars, in-range glyph ids and one variation value. PASSES.
+
+10. **`outline_glyph_bbox_encloses_emitted_points`** — when `outline_glyph`
+    returns a bbox, every emitted point (including Bezier control points, which
+    the library's bbox also uses) lies within it (±1 unit for the f32→i16
+    truncation in `RectF::to_rect`), and `min <= max`. Exercises the gvar path
+    too. Evidence: `outline_glyph` "returns its tight bounding box" + the
+    glyf/CFF builders extending bbox by every point. PASSES.
+
+11. **`set_variation_panics_on_non_finite_values_known_failure`** — canonical
+    failing test for bug #2. Deterministically draws NaN/±inf.
+    **FAILS by design.**
+
+12. **`face_height_overflows_i16_known_failure`** — canonical failing test for
+    bug #3. Builds a minimal valid font whose ascender/descender difference
+    exceeds `i16::MAX`. **FAILS by design.**
+
+### Evidence the suite is live
+
+- Liveness: temporarily inverting the assertion in
+  `tag_to_bytes_from_bytes_roundtrip` produced a shrunk counterexample
+  (`n = 0`), confirming cases are generated and assertions are checked. Reverted;
+  `.hegel/` deleted.
+- Exploratory 10x run: all nine passing properties re-run at `test_cases = 1000`
+  found no new failures (only the three known bugs). Reverted to defaults.
+
+## Test results
+
+`cargo test` (debug): **158 passed, 3 failed** — the 3 failures are the three
+canonical bug tests (#7, #11, #12). The 149 pre-existing tests plus the 9
+passing hegel properties all pass. Failures are deterministic across repeated
+runs.
+
+Release mode (`cargo test --release --test tables hegel_props`): **10 passed,
+2 failed**. Bug #1 (glyph 0) and bug #3 (`height` overflow) still fail — #3
+returns a *wrong wrapped value* (-32767 instead of 32769) rather than panicking.
+Bug #2 (`set_variation` non-finite) passes in release because it is a
+debug-assert-only panic.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### 1. `glyph_index` returns `Some(GlyphId(0))`, violating its documented contract
+
+- **Minimal counterexample:** on the pristine, unmodified `tests/fonts/demo.ttf`,
+  `face.glyph_index('\u{FFFF}')` returns `Some(GlyphId(0))`.
+- **Why it's a library bug:** `Face::glyph_index` is documented "Returns `None`
+  instead of `0` when glyph is not found" (src/lib.rs). Formats 0 and 2, and the
+  `id_range_offset != 0` branch of format 4, all contain explicit "glyph is
+  zero → return None" checks, showing the authors intend this invariant
+  everywhere.
+- **Code path:** `Face::glyph_index` → cmap subtable format 4
+  (`src/tables/cmap/format4.rs`) `id_range_offset == 0` branch:
+  ```rust
+  if id_range_offset == 0 {
+      return Some(GlyphId(code_point.wrapping_add(id_delta as u16)));
+  }
+  ```
+  This branch returns the computed id **without** the `if glyph == 0 { None }`
+  check its sibling glyph-array branch has. demo.ttf's format-4 subtable has the
+  standard end-of-table sentinel segment `start == end == 0xFFFF`,
+  `idDelta == 1`, `idRangeOffset == 0`, so U+FFFF maps to `0xFFFF + 1 == 0`.
+  Reproduces identically in debug and release.
+
+### 2. `Face::set_variation` panics on non-finite `value` (debug builds)
+
+- **Minimal counterexample:** `face.set_variation(axis, f32::NAN)` (also `+inf`,
+  `-inf`) on any variable face (`tests/fonts/colr_1_variable.ttf`) panics in a
+  debug build: `assertion failed: val.is_finite()` at `src/parser.rs:916`.
+- **Why it's a library bug:** `set_variation` documents no restriction on
+  `value`; a public setter should not panic on a valid `f32`.
+- **Code path:** `Face::set_variation` → `VariationAxis::normalized_value`
+  (src/tables/fvar.rs) → `parser::f32_bound`, whose
+  `debug_assert!(val.is_finite())` fires. Release builds compile the assert out
+  and instead silently produce a NaN/clamped coordinate, so the input is not
+  really validated either way — the underlying issue is that non-finite input
+  is never rejected.
+
+### 3. `Face::height()` (and `vertical_height()`) overflow `i16`
+
+- **Minimal counterexample:** a valid font with hhea `ascender = 16384`,
+  `descender = -16385`. `face.height()` panics in debug (`attempt to subtract
+  with overflow`, `src/lib.rs:1599`) and in release wraps to `-32767` instead of
+  the correct `32769`.
+- **Why it's a library bug:** `height()` returns `i16` but computes
+  `self.ascender() - self.descender()`, a difference that can exceed `i16`
+  range for values the font format permits (ascender/descender are independent
+  `i16`s). No documentation warns the result can be wrong/panic.
+- **Code path:** `Face::height` = `self.ascender() - self.descender()`
+  (src/lib.rs:1599); `Face::vertical_height` (src/lib.rs:1705) has the identical
+  unchecked subtraction.
+
+None of these were fixed, per instructions. The three canonical tests are left
+failing and pinned deterministically; sibling properties exclude exactly the
+buggy input regions (with comments referencing the canonical tests) so they keep
+exercising everything else.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None. Every property drafted survived scrutiny. Two required *narrowing an
+argument range* to protect sibling no-panic properties from an input that is
+itself a pinned bug (finite-only variation values in `exercise_face` /
+`outline_glyph_bbox_encloses_emitted_points`, and skipping `height()` /
+`vertical_height()` when the i16 subtraction would overflow). These are bug
+exclusions, not unsound properties — each is documented and each bug has its own
+canonical failing test.
+
+The bbox-enclosure property (#10) needed a genuine domain relaxation, not an
+abandonment: points may sit up to 1 font unit outside the returned bbox because
+`RectF::to_rect` truncates the f32 min/max toward zero when converting to `i16`.
+This tolerance is documented in the test.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+The skill and the Rust reference were accurate and sufficient; everything
+compiled and ran following them. Specific notes:
+
+- **MSRV gotcha hit exactly as documented.** `cargo add --dev hegeltest` failed
+  with the MSRV resolution error; the reference's "retry with
+  `--ignore-rust-version`" fix worked first time. Good call-out.
+
+- **Edition 2018 `TryFrom`/`TryInto` import.** ttf-parser is edition 2018, so
+  `try_into()`/`try_from()` in test code needed explicit
+  `use std::convert::{TryFrom, TryInto};`. This is an edition-2018 std-prelude
+  fact rather than a hegel issue, but since the reference already has a
+  dedicated "Edition 2015 crates" note and mentions edition ≤2018 quirks around
+  `assert!` format strings, a one-line reminder that 2018 test code may need the
+  `TryFrom`/`TryInto` imports would save a compile cycle.
+
+- **`draw_char` / codepoint idiom.** The reference's "Characters and Codepoints"
+  section (draw `u32`, `char::from_u32(...).unwrap_or('\u{FFFD}')`, weight planes
+  with `one_of!`) transferred directly and was exactly what a font parser needs.
+  Boosting a specific known-bad codepoint (U+FFFF) via a `one_of!`/boolean split
+  to pin a bug deterministically matched the "Keeping a Failing Test" guidance
+  well.
+
+- **Helper functions taking `&TestCase`.** Building the whole `exercise_face`
+  driver and the `draw_font_shaped` wire-format generator as plain
+  `fn(&hegel::TestCase)` helpers (per the reference's "Helper functions taking
+  `&TestCase`" note) was the right tool — no composite/`flat_map` gymnastics
+  needed for heavily dependent, imperative generation. This is a real strength
+  of the imperative API and the docs sell it accurately.
+
+- **No surprises in stateful/collection/one_of APIs.** `sampled_from` over owned
+  tuples, `binary().min_size(n)` with a separately-drawn `n`, `arrays`,
+  `one_of!` with mixed generator types — all behaved as documented.
